        {
          "claim": {
            "notes": "Reconciles legacy research-prototype claim ab1f826b-aecf-4f2b-a88a-bccf1eea4f9a into current post-M1 semantics. Related broader workbook-migrated claim 85bb684d-0aad-5410-a466-d3aad7f49c6f remains separate lineage. No new P-level is assigned. Generic Reich/modern-country practice polygon is rejected; authoritative forced-labour sites are eligible as evidence loci and historically dated jurisdiction geometry requires separate review.",
            "summary": "From 1942 through 1944, Nazi Germany operated a centrally directed, racially differentiated forced-labour system drawing on deported foreign civilians, prisoners of war, concentration-camp prisoners and other persecuted groups. The 21 March 1942 decree centralized labour deployment under Fritz Sauckel, specialist scholarship documents the mass foreign-labour regime, and USHMM synthesis records more than 7.5 million non-German workers registered in the Reich in August 1944, the overwhelming majority forced labourers. The atlas records a strong positive state-forced-labour claim without converting workforce totals into territorial prevalence or equating every coerced status with chattel slavery.",
